    "html": "<p>A language model feature can look excellent in a carefully selected demo and still disappoint in everyday use. Users phrase questions differently, source data is incomplete, integrations fail, and some requests should not receive an answer at all.</p>\n<p>Evaluation makes these differences visible. It does not have to start with a complicated benchmark platform; it needs representative examples, clear expectations, and a routine for learning from failures.</p>\n<h2>Create a useful set of examples</h2>\n<p>Collect real or carefully constructed tasks that reflect how people will use the feature. Include straightforward requests, ambiguous wording, missing information, outdated or conflicting sources, malformed inputs, and requests outside the feature’s intended scope.</p>\n<p>For each example, record what a good result should do. Sometimes there is an exact expected field or citation. Sometimes there are several acceptable answers, but the response still needs to be grounded, complete, and appropriately cautious.</p>\n<h2>Combine checks instead of chasing one score</h2>\n<p>Use deterministic checks for what software can verify consistently: output schemas, required values, valid links, permission filters, and whether a requested escalation occurred. Add human review for relevance, clarity, factual support, and usefulness where automated scoring is not reliable enough.</p>\n<p>For retrieval applications, measure whether the right evidence appears before judging the generated answer. For agents, measure whether the agent chose permitted tools, stayed within its step limits, and stopped or escalated when it should.</p>\n<h2>Treat evaluation as regression testing</h2>\n<p>Save the examples and run them whenever the model, prompt, retrieval logic, data source, tool, or application changes. Compare results against a known baseline. A change can improve average quality while introducing a serious failure in one important workflow, so review examples by category rather than relying on one overall score.</p>\n<p>Track the cost and latency of evaluations too. A useful release decision balances quality with the time and operational resources required to deliver it.</p>\n<h2>Learn from production safely</h2>\n<p>Feedback and incidents can reveal cases that were missing from the evaluation set. Add representative versions of those cases, while following privacy and retention rules and removing unnecessary personal details. Do not turn production conversations into training or evaluation data without appropriate rights and safeguards.</p>\n<p>Evaluation is most effective when it is part of regular engineering work: define expectations, test realistic cases, inspect failures, and make careful changes. The point is not to prove that a model is perfect. It is to understand where a feature is dependable, where it needs boundaries, and how the next release can improve it.</p>",
