    "html": "<p>Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is often reduced to a diagram: split documents into chunks, embed them, search a vector database, and send the results to a language model. That can produce a convincing demo. A useful product needs much more than the vector index.</p>\n<p>The system has to find the right evidence, respect who is allowed to see it, explain where an answer came from, and remain maintainable as the underlying information changes.</p>\n<h2>Start with the information itself</h2>\n<p>Before selecting an embedding model or database, map the sources people actually use. A policy may exist in a document repository, a support tool, and an old shared drive at the same time. Decide which sources are authoritative, how often they change, and what metadata is needed to filter results by team, tenant, date, or document status.</p>\n<p>Ingestion should be repeatable. Keep identifiers that let you update or remove a source without rebuilding everything. Track failures and freshness so operators can tell whether the index reflects the current source material.</p>\n<h2>Retrieval quality is more than chunk size</h2>\n<p>Chunking affects what context reaches the model, but it is only one part of retrieval. Useful results may combine keyword matching with semantic search, metadata filters, reranking, and query rewriting. The right combination depends on the language users ask in and how the source content is structured.</p>\n<p>Build a small evaluation set from real questions and known-good sources. Measure whether relevant documents appear in the top results before asking whether generated answers sound fluent. When retrieval misses, inspect the query, filters, source metadata, and ranking separately.</p>\n<h2>Treat access control as a retrieval rule</h2>\n<p>Permissions must apply before evidence is sent to the model—not only after the answer is generated. Carry the user’s authorization context into retrieval and test with accounts that should and should not see each source. A correct answer based on unauthorized information is still a failure.</p>\n<p>For sensitive use cases, also consider data retention, provider settings, audit needs, and what should or should not be included in logs. Those decisions belong in the system design rather than in a late-stage prompt.</p>\n<h2>Make answers verifiable</h2>\n<p>Show citations or clear source references when users need to validate an answer. Handle weak retrieval explicitly: ask a clarifying question, state that the information was not found, or route the request to a person. Do not let the model confidently fill gaps with plausible-sounding guesses.</p>\n<p>Track answer quality, citation correctness, no-answer behavior, latency, and cost. Review failure examples with the people who know the domain. That feedback helps distinguish a retrieval issue from stale source content, ambiguous wording, or a missing product capability.</p>\n<h2>Build the whole feedback loop</h2>\n<p>A maintainable RAG application includes source connectors, permissions, indexing, retrieval, generation, evaluation, monitoring, and a process for correcting bad answers. The vector database is an important component, but not the product.</p>\n<p>When a RAG system is designed around trusted sources and measurable user tasks, the team can improve it methodically—and know whether each change made it more helpful, safer, and more dependable.</p>",
    "slug": "rag-is-a-product-system",
    "readingTime": 3
